{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Abraham gave all that he had to Isaac.</b> He transferred the inheritance from all his sons, for so Sarah desired in saying ״כִּי לֹא יִירַשׁ בֶּן הָאָמָה״ and so forth (Genesis 21:10), and the Holy One, blessed be He, agreed with her and said ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר תֹּאמַר אֵלֶיךָ שָׂרָה שְׁמַע בְּקֹלָהּ, כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ (Genesis 21:12). And what he specified as ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״ — that is, all that Abraham acquired through the exertion of his strength and his labor, which came to him with the sweat of his nose and the toil of his hands — this is the thing uniquely his own, for ״מִי יֹאכַל וּמִי יָחוּשׁ חוּץ מִמֶּנִּי״ (Ecclesiastes 2:25) — all this he gave to Isaac. But to the sons of the concubines — that is, the sons of Hagar, for ״פִּלַגְשִׁם״ is written deficient — therefore he gave them those gifts that he had received from the Egyptians through Sarah, when he said ״אֲחֹתִי הִיא״ (Genesis 12:13), and then they gave him gifts; and those gifts he gave to the sons of Hagar the Egyptian, for it is just that from the place whence they came, there they return to go, and they went back to their planting-ground." "Another matter: that he gave Isaac his portion in the World to Come, and that is called ״אֶת כָּל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״, for what Abraham chose as his portion is the reward of the World to Come, as he was promised in the verse ״שְׂכָרְךָ הַרְבֵּה מְאֹד״ (Genesis 15:1), and this is called ״אֶת כָּל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״ absolutely, with no strangers sharing in it — to exclude that which a man holds of things handed from hand to hand, which is not his absolutely, for it is possible that strangers will plunder his toil. And so Monbaz said (Bava Batra 11a): ״My fathers stored up in a place where the hand reaches, but I stored up in a place where the hand does not reach״ — meaning: my treasure is laid up for the World to Come, a place where the hand of man does not reach. And furthermore, it is possible that in the middle of his days he will leave to others wealth and a wall which he built and which he planted; therefore the reward of this world is not his absolutely; only the reward of the World to Come is his alone. And this portion he gave to Isaac, that he too should receive his portion in the World to Come, for the reward of a mitzvah for the righteous does not exist in this world. But to the sons of the concubines he gave gifts — a thing handed from hand to hand — meaning that they receive their portion in this world, in the manner stated (Deuteronomy 7:10): ״וּמְשַׁלֵּם לְשׂנְאָיו אֶל פָּנָיו לְהַאֲבִידוֹ״, that if a wicked person performs some mitzvah, the Holy One, blessed be He, gives him his reward paid in full in this world. And it may be that on this account Sarah said in prophecy: ״כִּי לֹא יִירַשׁ בֶּן הָאָמָה הַזֹּאת עִם בְּנִי יִצְחָק״ (Genesis 21:10) — for at first glance it appears that she should have said simply ״כִּי לֹא יִירַשׁ בֶּן הָאָמָה זוֹ מִמְּךָ״; rather, she wanted to say by this that it is indeed fitting that the son of the maidservant should also inherit, but ״לֹא יִירַשׁ עִם בְּנִי״ — equally together — for the inheritances are not equal: the son of the maidservant will inherit his portion in this world, as written (Genesis 21:13): ״וְגַם אֶת בֶּן הָאָמָה לְגוֹי אֲשִׂימֶנּוּ״, that is, his portion in this world; but Isaac will receive his portion in the eternal world, after Ishmael has already received his portion. And this is what it says: ״וַיְשַׁלְּחֵם מֵעַל יִצְחָק בְּנוֹ קֵדְמָה״ — that they were sent ahead (מֻקְדָּמִים) in the receiving of their reward, in the manner stated concerning Jacob (Genesis 25:26): ״וְיָדוֹ אֹחֶזֶת בַּעֲקֵב עֵשָׂו״, that the hand of his rule will grasp at the end of Esau's boundary, hinted at in עָקֵב which is the end of the רֶגֶל, meaning that after the רֶגֶל of his pride in the marketplace of this world is consumed, then the rule of Jacob begins; and so it turns out that Esau is sent ahead (מֻקְדָּם) in receiving his reward. So too the sons of the concubines he sent קֵדְמָה to send them ahead in receiving their reward in this world — a thing handed from hand to hand — to exclude the reward of the World to Come, to enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah, for the term נְתִינָה does not apply to it."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The reading 'חוּץ מִמֶּנִּי' in Ecclesiastes 2:25 follows the Kli Yakar's citation, which reflects a known variant tradition of that verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:09.935476669Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}